# 2. Background and Related Work

## 2.1 Automated Prompt Optimization

Prompt optimization treats the prompt itself as an object to be optimized according to downstream model performance. Rather than updating model parameters, these methods modify natural-language instructions, demonstrations, reasoning scaffolds, or other components of the model input.

Early approaches such as ProTeGi use textual gradients or natural-language feedback to iteratively revise prompts [REF]. Subsequent methods have expanded the optimization process through automatic proposal generation, search, evaluation, and model-based reflection.

MIPROv2 combines prompt optimization with automated generation and selection of instruction and demonstration candidates [REF]. TextGrad formulates textual components as optimizable objects and propagates natural-language feedback through computational graphs [REF]. GEPA performs evolutionary-style prompt optimization using language-model-generated reflections and validation-based selection [REF]. Trace2Policy and related approaches use failure traces to identify behavioral patterns and iteratively refine instructions [REF].

These approaches demonstrate that language models can serve not only as task solvers but also as optimizers of the instructions used to solve those tasks.

Our work does not propose another generic search strategy. Instead, we investigate a specific feedback signal that can be incorporated into iterative prompt optimization: **the measured outcome of the optimizer's previous intervention**.

---

## 2.2 Failure Feedback Versus Intervention Feedback

A distinction central to our method is the difference between **failure feedback** and **intervention feedback**.

Failure feedback describes the state of the current candidate:

```text
Example 17 failed because ...
Example 31 failed because ...
Example 42 failed because ...
```

Intervention feedback additionally describes the transition caused by the previous candidate:

```text
Example 17:
previously incorrect -> now correct

Example 31:
previously correct -> now incorrect

Example 42:
incorrect -> incorrect
```

The latter contains information about the behavioral consequences of a specific prompt modification.

Formally, let \(p_t\) denote the prompt at optimization round \(t\), \(x_i\) an evaluation item, and \(y_i\) its expected output. Let

$$
f(p_t, x_i)
$$

denote the solver's outcome on item \(i\), and define

$$
s_{t,i} =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } f(p_t,x_i)=y_i,\\
0 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

A conventional failure signal exposes information such as

$$
\mathcal{F}_t = \{x_i : s_{t,i}=0\}.
$$

Reflective-FDPO additionally computes the transition

$$
\Delta_{t,i}=s_{t,i}-s_{t-1,i}.
$$

This partitions examples into:

$$
\mathcal{R}_t = \{i:\Delta_{t,i}=+1\}
$$

for recovered items and

$$
\mathcal{G}_t = \{i:\Delta_{t,i}=-1\}
$$

for regressed items.

The optimizer therefore receives information about both the current state and the observed effect of its previous intervention.

