# 9. Evaluation Metrics

We measure both **task performance** and **optimization efficiency**.

## 9.1 Task Performance

For accuracy-based benchmarks:

$$
\text{Accuracy} =
\frac{\text{correct predictions}}
{\text{total predictions}}.
$$

For PUPA, we use the benchmark's composite metric:

$$
S_{\text{PUPA}}
=
\frac{\text{quality}+(1-\text{leakage})}{2}.
$$

For IFEval/IFBench, we use the mechanically verified pass-rate implementation described in Appendix [X].

---

## 9.2 Optimization Gain

For a method \(m\):

$$
\Delta_m =
S(p_m^*) - S(p_0).
$$

To directly compare Reflective-FDPO with Simple-FDPO:

$$
\Delta_{\text{reflect-vs-simple}}
=
S(p_{\text{Reflective}}^*)
-
S(p_{\text{Simple}}^*).
$$

---

## 9.3 Recovery and Regression

For each optimization round, we compute:

$$
R_t = |\{i:s_{t-1,i}=0,\ s_{t,i}=1\}|
$$

and

$$
G_t = |\{i:s_{t-1,i}=1,\ s_{t,i}=0\}|.
$$

We define the net transition as:

$$
N_t = R_t-G_t.
$$

This provides a direct measure of whether a prompt revision produces more recoveries than regressions.

---

## 9.4 Optimization Efficiency

We additionally report:

* optimizer calls;
* solver calls;
* total tokens;
* optimizer tokens;
* solver tokens;
* wall-clock time;
* estimated monetary cost;
* improvement per optimization round; and
* improvement per dollar.

For each configuration:

$$
\text{Efficiency}
=
\frac{\Delta}{\text{optimization cost}}.
$$

