## 14.2 Round Selection

An optimization trajectory can contain several candidate prompts:

$$
p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_T.
$$

Selecting the final candidate \(p_T\) is not necessarily optimal.

We therefore evaluate a **best-of-rounds** policy that selects:

$$
p^* = \arg\max_{p_t} S_{\text{validation}}(p_t).
$$

The selected prompt is then evaluated once on the sealed test set.

This policy uses validation information that is already collected during optimization rather than discarding it after each round.

The preliminary experiments motivating this change found cases where a later round had lower validation performance than an earlier committed round. However, a retroactive PUPA analysis showed that one such validation-based choice did not produce a statistically meaningful test improvement by itself.

We therefore treat best-of-round selection as an engineering improvement rather than evidence of a standalone causal effect.

