# 23. Conclusion

We introduced Reflective Feedback-Driven Prompt Optimization, a prompt optimization procedure that explicitly exposes an optimizer to the measured consequences of its previous prompt revisions.

The central difference from conventional failure-driven optimization is simple:

> **Instead of showing the optimizer only what failed, we show it what changed because of its previous intervention.**

Through a controlled Simple-FDPO versus Reflective-FDPO comparison, we investigate whether this additional feedback improves prompt optimization across five heterogeneous tasks and multiple solver models.

Our final results show that Reflective-FDPO [IMPROVES / DOES NOT CONSISTENTLY IMPROVE] performance across [X] of [TOTAL] configurations, with an average improvement of [X.X] percentage points over Simple-FDPO. The effect is [MODEL-/TASK-DEPENDENT], indicating that outcome-aware feedback is [a broadly useful / a conditionally useful] component of automated prompt optimization.

Beyond the optimization results themselves, our repeated-run experiments demonstrate that prompt optimization can be substantially affected by LLM sampling variance and small evaluation-set sizes. We therefore argue that multi-seed evaluation should be treated as a first-class requirement for empirical prompt-optimization research rather than an optional robustness experiment.

More broadly, our findings suggest that prompt optimization can benefit from treating each prompt revision as an intervention whose consequences should be measured and fed back into subsequent optimization. This perspective shifts automated prompt optimization from simply **diagnosing failures** toward **learning from the behavioral consequences of previous edits**.

